3.14. COMPUTATIONAL APPLICATIONS 113
in 3-dimensional space, which is the intersection of three surfaces, we can
compute the dimension of the ideal. Moreover, we can check the number of
the associated prime ideals by the commend “size(minAssGTZ(I))”. In this
example, we can confirm that the ideal has only one associate prime, hence
I is a prime, and the variety is irreducible.
ring r=0,(x,y,z),dp;
ideal I=y2-xz,x2y-z2,x3-yz;
dim(groebner(I));
==> 1
ideal J=lead(groebner(I));
J;
==> J[1]=y2
==> J[2]=x2y
==> J[3]=x3
dim(groebner(J));
==> 1
LIB "primdec.lib";
size(minAssGTZ(I));
==> 1
